                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

We identified during the blower door test that almost all of the air leakage of your home is associated with the many heating appliances installed (read my section on Heating Systems for additional details). There was also quite a bit of air leakage from the floor of the sauna room, around the door opening into the sauna room, the trim around the entrance door to the master bedroom, around the older windows, the wire through the wall of the rear workshop, and the weather-stripping around the two oldest exterior doors.



The current total air leakage from your home is measured at over 13 "air changes per hour" (ACH) when there is a 50 Pascal (Pa) pressure difference between the inside &amp; outside of the house. 50 Pa represents about a 32 km/hr wind, which is often experienced during the winter heating season. This means that all of the interior air is drawn out 13 times per hour, or about once every 4 minutes !!



I would estimate that, if you can consolidate your heating &amp; hot water requirements into one high-efficiency boiler (thus eliminating all of the current chimneys in your house) and you are able to address most of the other air leakage locations identified above, you should be able to reduce the air leakage from your home by 50%.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 50% reduction in air leakage you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,669&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;56.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <windows>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the older windows that you are planning to upgrade (the ones you marked with some green tape). Similar to the other newer windows in your house, I recommend ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing these 7 older wood windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 225&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

The domestic hot water for your house is currently supplied by a dedicated loop from the oil boiler, incorporating a 41 USG Amtrol Boilermate storage tank. The hot water heating component is estimated to operate a 0.57 Efficiency Factor (EF) - approximately the annual percentage of heat that is delivered to the hot water verses what percentage goes up the chimney or is lost through short cycling inefficiencies of the boiler (especially during the summer months).



Please look at my upgrade recommendation to the current oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) - the average efficiency over the whole heating season, not just the peak efficiency. Using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (still using the Amtrol Boilermate storage tank) will increase the hot water efficiency to 0.79 EF.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

The estimated annual savings have been incorporated into the savings of the boiler upgrade, so please have a look at that section of my report.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house currently has 5 different heating systems:



1. The Oil Drip stove in the kitchen area - used as a primary system

-    6" chimney , with a dedicated &amp; connected outside combustion air supply pipe



2. The Empire wall furnace in the rear addition - also used as a primary system

-    5" chimney, and draws its' combustion air from inside the house



3. The Hunter wall furnace in the rear left family room area - used as required

-    Direct Vent "concentric" pipe through the wall, drawing combustion air from outside the house



4. The Arden wood stove in the right addition living area - used as required

-    7" chimney, with a dedicated &amp; connected outside combustion air supply pipe



5. The small wood stove in the sauna.

-    5" chimney, and draws its' combustion air from inside the house



All of the above heating systems have very low combustion efficiency, hovering around 70% at best. One of the key things we noticed is the high level of air leakage associated with chimneys of all of these heating systems, especially that Empire wall furnace. The heating appliance that was best sealed is the wood stove.



Speaking about chimney air leakage, we also noticed a very high amount coming from the new on-demand hot water unit - both around the 5" chimney as it passes through the ceiling and from the air that will continuously flow through the hot water unit and up through the chimney itself ........ even when the unit is not operating.



The 5" chimneys for the Empire wall furnace, the on-demand hot water and the 6" chimney for the oil drip stove are all functioning like BIG holes in your house, constantly drawing inside air outside, especially on windy days. The on-demand hot water unit is located near the top of your house ....... drawing the warmest air outside.



I have thought about what alternate mechanical systems designs would help increase the efficiency, and reduce air leakage. I am thinking you could install a 95% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), ENERGY STAR® qualified condensing propane boiler, that has various zones throughout your house, and even a zone dedicated to your domestic hot water, with a small buffer tank installed. This would allow you to eliminate all of the heating appliance listed above, plus the on-demand hot water system.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing all of your heating systems with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 2,554&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;85.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for the new boiler.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Using Biomass - Wood Stove &lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has a wood stove that appears to have a dedicated combustion air supply (ie: it obtains combustion air from outside the house).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

I have estimated that if you use your current wood stove to provide 75% of the heating requirements for about 1000 sq. ft. of your house (about 60% of the total interior heated floor space on all floors of your house) at the "self-harvested" wood rate of about $100 per cord (covers personal chainsaw, gas, truck expenses), you would reduce your annual heating costs by $2,836 more per year than my estimated savings within the Heating System section of this report.



If you decide to purchase &amp; install a new qualified wood stove (with a dedicated outside combustion air supply), you could qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has no mechanical ventilation (HRV, Range Hood, Bathroom Fan or a Clothes Dryer) that vents to the outside of the house. You therefore manage the ventilation requirement by opening a window or door when required. Based on the current air leakage level of your home ...... this approach appears to work OK.



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan in the upper bathroom that is connected to a humidistat, or a timer switch. This will help control excess moisture within the bathrooms, and the newer ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fans will incorporate a built-in damper to reduce any air leakage when it is not operating. Some folks will wire a switch on the main floor to that bathroom fan so it can be turned on to ventilate the house during the spring/summer/fall.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



As there are no exhaust fans in your house, you are unable to create any negative pressure within the house that would compete with the chimneys of the oil drip stove, wood stove(s), or the 2 x propane wall furnaces. Even if you installed a bathroom fan or vented the range hood fan to the outside of the house, there is significant natural air leakage in your house that should not result in any interior depressurization that would compete with your chimneys. In addition, the oil drip stove, the wood stove &amp; the one Hunter wall furnace all appear to have dedicated outside combustion air pipes.



During my assessment of your home, I did not see any smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP)&lt;/b&gt;



You will notice that many of the upgrades opportunities outlined in this report will refer to the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) that the Arctic Energy Alliance administers. During my evaluation of your house, we talked about many other energy conservation and efficiency measures (eg: LED light bulbs or tubes, Programmable Thermostats, ECM pumps for boilers or water circ pumps, ESTAR refrigerators &amp; washing machines, etc.).



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For full program information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife. The costs for heating fuels will be similar at Pontoon Lake, however your electricity costs will be different with the various components of your self-generation system.

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</AdditionalComments>
